model
  schema 1.1

type user

type account
  relations
    define owner: [user]
    define viewer: [user] or owner

type opportunity
  relations
    define parent: [account]
    define viewer: [user] or viewer from parent
    define editor: [user] or owner from parent

type invoice
  relations
    define parent: [account]
    define viewer: [user] or viewer from parent
    define editor: [user] or owner from parent

type note
  relations
    define parent: [account]
    define viewer: [user] or viewer from parent
    define editor: [user] or owner from parent

# Day 2 model review — both questions resolved. See docs/DECISIONS.md.
#
# 1. The three child types are identical, which looks like duplication worth
#    collapsing into one "record" type with a type attribute. We keep them
#    separate. §5.3 puts the resource type inside the scope string
#    (system:type:action:id), and §7.1 and §7.2 both build the OpenFGA object
#    straight from it — `object=f"{rtype}:{rid}"`. One type per scope type means
#    that line stays a concatenation. Collapsing would put a translation table
#    on the enforcement path, which is the last place extra logic belongs.
#    Auditors also ask "who can view invoices"; separate types answer it
#    directly. The cost is three mechanical blocks, which is the cheaper half
#    of the trade.
#
# 2. The asymmetry is deliberate. `viewer` inherits from the parent's `viewer`,
#    but `editor` inherits from the parent's `owner`. Being able to read an
#    account does NOT let you write to things under it. §7.1 maps
#    action=="write" to relation "editor", so this line is what stops a
#    read-only analyst delegating write authority.
#
#    It is now enforced rather than remembered: deploy/seed/fga-checks.json
#    asserts that auditor@acme.test — a viewer on Northwind, never an owner —
#    is refused `editor` on its children. `make seed` and CI both run it.
#
# This file is the human-readable source of truth. deploy/seed/fga-model.json
# is the same model in the form the HTTP API accepts, because transpiling the
# DSL needs the `fga` CLI and we did not want that in everyone's path on Day 1.
# tests/test_policy_model.py asserts the two never drift apart.
